# WolkaraForever layer patch 0085 - host: a TouchPlay chord's modifiers stay down for the game's frame (iPad touch)
#
# Why: on the iPad (an iPad test, 16 Nov) every TouchPlay button reached the game without its modifiers: the spell
# holds (CTRL-ALT-SHIFT-H/J/K, -M) opened the game's own PvP, Guild, Professions and Map panels, Jump
# (CTRL-ALT-SHIFT-V) toggled the nameplates, the taps (F2..F8) did nothing. 0080 posted each chord as one batch -
# the modifiers' flags-changed events, the key, the modifiers' release - in a single main-thread call. The guest
# drains the whole queue before it runs a frame, and the game follows the modifiers as their events come but acts
# on a key in its frame, with the modifiers it holds then: by then the release had been taken too, so it saw the
# bare key. The simulator fixture's guest read each event's own flags, so it passed; its guest now acts in frames
# as the game does. A hardware keyboard never hits this: a held modifier spans many frames.
#   - touchKey:characters:modifiers:pressed: presses the modifiers a key needs (as 0080: a flags-changed event
#     with the key code and left-hand device bit) and the key, and keeps them down. They come up, in reverse, once
#     the guest has found the queue empty AKTouchChordReleasePolls (3) times since the chord's last key and at
#     least AKTouchChordReleaseSeconds (50 ms) has passed - a frame or more after the one that took the key,
#     however often the guest polls in a frame. A tap's down and up share one press; another chord key while they
#     are down keeps them down longer (a modifier it does not need comes up first).
#   - While they are down, every other event the host posts - a click, a walk key, a typed or hardware key, a
#     scroll - waits in order and follows the release, its flags stripped of the chord's: a world tap while Jump is
#     held is a plain click, the walk stick's W/A/S/D stay bare. A pointer move with nothing waiting goes at once
#     (an aimed spell's circle follows the finger). NSApplication asks the host holding a chord (postEvent:atStart:)
#     and tells it when the guest found the queue empty (nextEventMatchingMask:, once per call); what the release
#     posts is delivered in that same call.
#   - reconcileModifiers: keeps the chord's modifiers, which UIKit does not report, while they are held.
#   - The first eight releases are logged: polls, milliseconds and how many events waited (no keys, no text).
# Only TouchPlay's areas (iPad touch) send keys with modifiers, so no other interface ever holds a chord and the
# queue's two added checks are a nil test there.
#
# Later (an iPad test, 16 Nov): with this hold the keys still arrived bare, so timing was not the cause. The game
# builds a chord as ALT-CTRL-SHIFT-<key> and looks its binding up by that exact string; TouchPlay had bound
# CTRL-ALT-SHIFT-<key>, which was never found, so the game ran the bare key's binding. The add-on now binds in
# the game's order and the contract reader takes either (TouchContract.m). The hold is kept: it is
# harmless, and the guest log shows the game reads each event's own flags (it never calls CGEventSourceFlagsState
# or +[NSEvent modifierFlags]), so the events were right all along.
#
# Applies after: 0084-touchplay-leaving-the-app-lets-go-of-the-world.patch
# Applies to: translation/AppKit/AppKit.m
# Upstream: no (TouchPlay and iPad touch live in the WolkaraForever layer).
#
